## Why Gaudin differs from XXX {#sec:whydiff}

In XXX only the specific fused shapes $T_{(1,1)},T_{(2,1)},\dots$ localise; in Gaudin every Laurent coefficient in $D_1$ at $\theta_1$ localises on its own. This was the round-1 surprise, and we have no derivation of it. Our reading, which has not been tested, is that in the $h\to0$ limit the shifted transfer matrices at $\theta_s-mh$ collapse onto jets at $\theta_s$, so that the products needed for a fused shape reduce to combinations of Laurent coefficients, and the spectators become Laurent polynomials in $\theta_{12}$ with point-dependent constants instead of ladder products.
